3401:
** μῑμ-έομαι**,
_future_ -ήσομαι: _aorist_ ἐμιμησάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.21, etc.:}}_perfect_ μεμίμημαι (see. below):—**imitate, represent, portray**, ἔργα Γιγάντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 7; φωνάς “hymnus ad Apollinem” 163; γόον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.21; γλώσσης ἀϋτήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 564; τὴν τοῦ παιδὸς ὄρχησιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.21}}; τινα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 370, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.166, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1037, etc.}}; μ.τινά τι one **in** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67}}; τινὰ κατὰ τὰ αἰδοῖα uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.104; κατὰ φωνὴν ἢ κατὰ σχῆμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393c}}; [ὀρθὴν πολιτείαν] ἐπὶ τὰ καλλίω, ἐπὶ τὰ αἰσχίονα μεμιμημένας, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 293e; ἐπὶ τὸ σεμνόν [prev. author] “Lg.” 814e; ἡδοναὶ μεμιμημέναι τὰς ἀληθεῖς ἐπὶ τὰ γελοιότερα [prev. author] “Phlb.” 40c: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μιμήσεις πονηρὰς μ. τινά}} **imitate** him in what is bad, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 705c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1430, “Pl.” 306; τὰ πλεῖστα μ. τὴν Κρητικὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b22:}}_perfect_ _participle_ μεμιμημένος, in _active_ sense, στύλοισι φοίνικας μεμιμημένοισι pillars **made to represent** palms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.169, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 414b}}: in _passive_ sense, **made exactly like, portrayed**, γραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.78, 86, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1371b6}}: _present_ _participle_ in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604e:}}_future_ _participle_ μιμηθησόμενον{{Refs|[prev. work] 599a}}: _aorist_ _participle_ μιμηθέν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 668b.}}
**II** of the arts, **represent, express by means of imitation**, of an actor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 605c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 291 (Lyric poetry)}}; of painting and music, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 306d; τὴν τῶν μελῶν μίμησιν τὴν εὖ καὶ τὴν κακῶς μεμιμημένην [prev. author] “Lg.” 812c}}; of poetry, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1447a17, +others}}; of μῖμοι, **represent, act**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.21.}} —Neither μῖμος, μιμέομαι, nor any derivations occur in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” or 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}}:—Trag. use only _present_ and _future_ **μῑμ-ηλάζω**, = {μιμεία}, ἀγαθὸν κακῷ μ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.557 (assuming variant); -άζοντες καὶ παρακόπτοντες τὸ δόκιμον νόμισμα [prev. work] 610}} (-ίζοντες codices),{{Refs 5th c.AD+|compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

3402:
** μῑμ-ητής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**imitator**, τινων{{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. work = 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia”] 1.6.3, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.22, NT.1Cor.4.16}}; οἱ μ. τῶν γραμμάτων **forgers**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 115.4.}}
**II** **artist** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (μίμησις 11), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 602a, +others}}; especially **one who impersonates** characters, as an actor or poet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 918b28, “Po.” 1460a8.}}
**II.2** coupled with γόης, **mere actor, impostor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 598d, compare “Plt.” 303c, “Sph.” 235a.}}

3403:
** μιμνήσκω**
(not μιμν-ήσκω, see below), _future_ μνήσω: _aorist_ ἔμνησα: causal Verb, formed in _present_ and _imperfect_ from μέμνημαι as πιπράσκω from πέπραμαι:— **remind, put in mind**, μνήσει δέ σε καὶ θεὸς αὐτός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.38}}; τινος **of** a thing, ἐπεί μ᾽ ἔμνησας ὀϊζύος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.103; τῶν σ᾽ αὖτις μνήσω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.31, compare 1.407; μηδέ με τούτων μίμνησκ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.169, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1123, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.36.}}
**II** ἔμνασεν ἑστίαν πατρῴαν.. νικῶν **recalled** it **to memory, made** it **famous**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.13.}} —_active_ is mostly _Epic dialect_, used once in Trag. (Lyric poetry), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 878: compounds with ἀνα- or ὑπο- were preferred in Prose.}}
**B** _middle_ and _passive_ **μιμνήσκομαι**, _imperative_ -ήσκεο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.268}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ μιμνήσκοντο {{Refs|13.722}} (the _present_ only in later Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368a, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.13, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.653b}}; μέμνημαι serving as _present_ in early writers): other tenses are formed from the stem μνη- (see. μνάομαι): _future_ μνήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.192, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 32; μνησθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.19, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 933, etc.; also μεμνήσομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.390, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.581, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1461, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31b, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐμνησάμην, _infinitive_ μνήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.331, 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.1, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39}}; rare in Trag., as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 564}}; _Epic dialect_ μνησάσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.566}}; Trag. also ἐμνήσθην (used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.118), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 373, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἐμνάσθην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 4.11:}}_perfect_ μέμνημαι, _Aeolic dialect_ μέμναιμαι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 28.6}}, in _Attic dialect_ always in _present_ sense, as also frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ μέμνηαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.442, μέμνῃ 15.18}}; _imperative_ μέμνησο, _Doric dialect_ μέμνᾱσο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 250, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ μέμνεο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.105}}; _subjunctive_ μέμνωμαι -ώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.168, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 49}}; _Ionic dialect_ -εώμεθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?) in “PLit.Lond.” 54.4; _optative_ μεμνῄμην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.745, - ῇτο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 991 (μεμνῇο, -ῇτο should probably be read for -ῷο or -οῖο, -ῷτο in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.5, “Cyr.” 1.6.3, and μεμνοῖτο is uncertain in 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 50)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ μεμνέῳτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.361}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ μεμναίατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 94}}; _infinitive_ μεμνῆσθα; _Aeolic dialect_ _imperative_ μέμναισο {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.8}}; _participle_ μεμνημένος: _pluperfect_ ἐμεμνήμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.35}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐμεμνέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.104}}:—**remind oneself of** a thing, **call to mind**:—Constr.: sometimes with _accusative_, **remember**, Τυδέα δ᾽ οὐ μέμνημαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.222, compare 9.527, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.168, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1057, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 633d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.7; especially with _relative_ clause following, μ. τὸν στόλον ὡς ἔπρηξε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18; μέμνησο δ᾽ ἀμφίβληστρον ὡς ἐκαίνισας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 492; μ. τὸν Εὐφραῖον, οἷ᾽ ἔπαθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.61}}; also μέμνησο ἐκεῖνο, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.25}}; μεμνώμεθα ταῦτα περὶ ἀμφοῖν, ὅτι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31a: more frequently with _genitive_, φίλου μεμνήσομ᾽ ἑταίρου 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.390; τοῦ ποτε μεμνήσεσθαι ὀΐομαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.581; οὐδὲ παῖδος οὐδὲ φίλων τοκήων οὐδὲν ἐμνάσθη 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Supp.” [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1461, etc.; also μεμνημένος ἀμφ᾽ Ὀδυσῆϊ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.151; ἀμφὶ Διώνυσον.. μνήσομαι “Hymni Homerici” 7.2; περὶ πομπῆς μνησόμεθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.192}}:—_passive_, **to be remembered** (not in early Prose), τὰ παραπτώματα οὐ μνησθήσεται {{LXX+NT|LXX.Eze.18.22; αἱ ἐλεημοσύναι σου ἐμνήσθησαν NT.Act.10.31, cf. NT.Rev.16.19.}}
**B.2** with _infinitive_, μέμνηντο γὰρ αἰεὶ ἀλλήλοις.. ἀλεξέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.364; μέμνησο δ᾽ εἴκειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 202; μέμνησο δάκνειν, διαβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 495; μεμνήσθω ἀγαθὸς ἀνὴρ εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.39; μέμνησθέ μοι μὴ θορυβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27b.}}
**B.3** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, with}}_participle_, θνατὰ μεμνάσθω περιστέλλων μέλη **let him remember that** he clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.15}}; μέμνημαι κλύων I **remember** hearing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 830; μεμνήμεθα ἐλθόντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 244; μ. ἀκούσας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.3, etc.}}: followed by a _relative_, μέμνησ᾽, ὅπως εὖ μοι στομώσεις αὐτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1107.}}
**B.4** _absolutely_, ἀφ᾽ οὗ Ἕλληνες μέμνηνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, compare 5.66:}}_perfect_ _participle_ μεμνημένος in commands, ὧδέ τις.. μεμνημένος ἀνδρὶ μαχέσθω let him fight **with good heed, let him remember** to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.153, compare 5.263, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 422, etc.}}
**B.II** **make mention of**, with _genitive_, τῶν νῦν μοι μνῆσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.331}}; Μοῦσαι, μνησαίαθ᾽ ὅσοι ὑπὸ Ἴλιον ἦλθον (i. e. τῶν, ὅσοι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.492; also μνήσασθαι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39: frequently in}}_aorist_ _passive_ μνησθῆναι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.118, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 310; μνησθῆναι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, compare 9.45; περί τινος ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.47, compare 1.10, 37, etc.}}; μνησθεὶς ὑπὲρ τῆς εἰρήνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.21; μ. τινὸς πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.19}}: later with _dative_ _person_, **recall to** one's **memory, remind**, ἐμνήσθην σοι καὶ παρόντι περί.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 12.1 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 122.7, +others (3rd c.BC)}}: rarely with _accusative_, ταῦτα καὶ μακάρων ἐμέμναντ᾽ ἀγοραί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).29: _absolutely_, μευ μεμναμένω εἰ φιλέεις με}} **mentioning** your name to see if.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.28.}}
**B.III** **give heed to**, πατρὸς καὶ μητέρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.267}}; μ. βρώμης **give heed** to food, {{Refs|10.177}}; ὡς μεμνέῳτο δρόμου (variant{δρόμους}) that **he might give heed to** the running, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.361}}; μ. χάρμης, δαιτός, σίτου,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.222, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.246, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.129; μεμνᾶσθαι πολέμου τε καὶ μάχας 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.58; ἀοιδᾶς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 94}}. (_Aeolic dialect_ **μιμναίσκω** (not μιμνᾴσκω) {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.79, 178}}; but _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, _Attic dialect_ **μιμνήσκω** without ι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 15v. 35 (3rd c.BC, ὑπο-), “Inscription Magn.” 16.27 (early 2nd c.BC, [ἀνα-]), “SIG” 704 E 18 (Delph., late 2nd c.BC, ὑπο-), Did.in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.196}}; _cognate_ with Latin _memini_, etc.)

3407:
** μίσθιος**, α, ον,
**salaried, hired**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 16, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.283; λινόϋφος μ. “Sammelb.” 4299.14 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** _substantive_ μίσθιος, ὁ, **hired labourer, servant**, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Lev.19.13 (variant), “PCair.Zen.” 378.14 (_plural_, 3rd c.BC), “Supp.epigram” 2.569.28, 4.447.45 (Didyma, 2nd c.BC), NT.Luke.15.17, “PAmh.” 2.92.19}} (unless = **tenant**) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD): in _plural_}}**mercenaries**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.2.1.}}

3408:
** μισθός**, ὁ,
**hire**, μισθῷ ἔπι ῥητῷ for fixed **wages**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.445}}; μισθοῖο τέλος the end **of our hired service**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 450; μισθὸς.. εἰρημένος ἄρκιος ἔστω 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 370; θητεύειν ἐπὶ μισθῷ παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.137, compare 5.65; πείθειν ἐπὶ μ. [prev. author] 8.4; μισθοῦ ἕνεκα ὑπηρετεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.5.14: _genitive_ μισθοῦ}} **for hire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 560, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.124, 7.25, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.94; μ. στρατεύεσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.109.6}}; μισθὸν δοῦναι, διδόναι, πορίζειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 609, “HF” 19, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1019}}; ὡς ἐς ἑξήκοντα ναῦς μηνὸς μισθόν as a month's **pay**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.8; μισθοὺς μεγάλους ἔφερον 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 434, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 66; μ. λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.117, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 593, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.83; δέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 16; φέρεσθαι [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.4}}; μ. πράττεσθαι exact it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325b; μ. αἰτεῖν [prev. author] “R.” 345e}}; **hire**, μ. ὄνων, πλοίου, {{Refs|“PAmh.” 2.126.11, 37.}}
**b** especially at Athens, **pay, allowance** for public service, μ. δικαστικός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 299; μ. ἐκκλησιαστικός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 25}}; ὁ τῆς πρυτανείας μ. **pay** received during the prytany, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.123.}}
**2** physician's **fee**, μ. ἄρνυσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a36.}}
**II** generally, **recompense, reward**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.304, etc.; ἀρετῆς μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363d, cf. NT.Matt.5.12, etc.}}
**II.2** in bad sense, **requital**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1261, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 221; μ. ἀνδρὶ δυσσεβεῖ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1050}}. (Cf. Avest. **mīˇžda**-, Gothic _mizdō_, OSlavonic _m[icaron]zda_ 'pay', O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **meord, méd** 'meed'.)

3409:
** μισθ-όω**,
**let out for hire, farm out**, in _present_ and _imperfect_, **offer to let**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 958, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.52}}; μισθοῖ αὑτὸν Ὀλυνθίοις **offers** his services **for pay** to them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.150}}, compare 149; ἑαυτὸν ἐπί τι for a purpose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.21; μ. τὸ τέμενος “IG” 12.94.5; οἶκον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.15, 28.15; τοὺς οἴκους τῶν ὀρφανῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.7: with}}_infinitive_, μ. τὸν νηὸν τριηκοσίων ταλάντων ἐξεργάσασθαι **farm out** the building of it for {{Refs 5th c.BC+|300 talents, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.180; ὅσου τὴν τριηραρχίαν ἦσαν μεμισθωκότες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.80.}}
**II** _middle_, _future_ μισθώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 52:}}_aorist_ ἐμισθωσάμην: _perfect_ μεμίσθωμαι (see. below):—**have let to one, hire**, with _accusative_ _person_ vel _of things_, πλοῖον, μισθωτούς, ἐπικουρικόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1152, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.52; οἰκίαν ἣν ᾤκει μεμισθωμένος 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.39}}; τίνα τῶν πολιτῶν ἑταιρεῖν μεμίσθωμα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.79; τῆς οἰκίας ἧς ἐμεμίσθωτο 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11; μισθωσάμενός τινος τὴν οἰκίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.58; μ. τι παρά τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68; τοὺς μεμισθωμένους παρ᾽ ἐμοῦ τὸ χωρίον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.8}}; μ. τινὰ ταλάντου **engage** his services at a talent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.131}}; ὀλίγου at a low price, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259a13: with}}_infinitive_, μ. νηὸν ἐξοικοδομῆσαι **contract** for the building of the temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.62}}; μ. γράψαι ψάφισμα Maiuri **Nuova Silloge** {{Refs|432 (from Cos)}}; μισθοῦσθαί τινα with _infinitive_, **hire** him to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.34; μισθοῦ σαυτὸν ἀμφορεαφορεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 299, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.33}}; μ. ὑπέρ τινος **make a contract** for a thing, {{Refs|[prev. author] 53.21}}; ὁ μισθωσάμενος the **contractor**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.34.}}
**III** _passive_, _aorist_ ἐμισθώθην: _perfect_ μεμίσθωμαι (see. below):—**to be hired for pay**, Μαρδονίῳ μεμισθωμένος οὐκ ὀλίγου **hired** by him at no small price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.38}}; ἐπί τινι for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.1}}; ἐκ τοῦ μισθωθῆναι from the **hire**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.58}}; of a house, **to be let on contract**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 28.1}}; of a mercenary, μισθωθεὶς ὑπό τινος {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.149.}}

3410:
** μίσθ-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**price agreed on in hiring, contract-price**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.180, etc.: _plural_, “IG” 12.347.43, +others (5th c.BC), 22.334.28 (4th c.BC)}}; a courtesan's **price**, Machoap.{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.581a; τῶν ἀκροαμάτων τὰ μ. 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 66 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τὰ παρά τινος μ. 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.37.}}
**2** **contract**, μισθοῦσι μισθώματα farm out **contracts**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 47.2, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.125}}; ἀπὸ μισθωμάτων θύειν by **contract**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.29}} (but explained as from the proceeds of **rents** by {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus).}}
**3** **rent,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(7).55.15 (Amorgos, 4th-3rd c.BC), “Tab.Heracl.” 1.128.}}
**II** **that which is let for hire, hired house,** {{NT|NT.Acts.28.30.}}

3411:
** μισθ-ωτός**, ή, όν,
**hired**, ἐπίκουροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 419; ἄνθρωποι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.67 K.}}
**II** _substantive_, **hireling, hired servant**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1152, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 918b, “IG” 22.1672.28, NT.Mark.1.20, etc.}}: frequently of soldiers, **mercenaries**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.6}}; of a spy or agent, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.38; μ. Φιλίππου [prev. work]52; καλὸς κἀγαθὸς καὶ δίκαιος μ. ἐκείνῳ [prev. author] 19.110.}}

3415:
** μνάομαι**,
_contraction_ **μνῶμαι**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes (only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”) in the _contraction_ forms μνᾶται, μνώμεθα, μνῶνται, μνάσθω, μνᾶσθαι, μνώμενος}}; compare _imperfect_ μνᾶτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 96.1: sometimes in these _contraction_ forms lengthened again, as _2nd pers._ _sing._}}_present_ μνάᾳ, _infinitive_ μνάασθαι [μνᾱ-], _participle_ μνωόμενο; _Ionic dialect_ μνώμενος [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96 (variant{μνεώμενος}), 205: _3rd.pers._ _plural_}}]_imperfect_ μνώοντο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below); _imperative_ μνώεο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.896, +others; _optative_ μνώοιο 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 74}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ μνάσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.290: only}}_present_ and _imperfect_, except _aorist_ μνήσατο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(in signification 11.1) 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 413.}}
**I** **to be mindful of**, with _genitive_, οὐ πολέμοιο ἐμνώοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.686; μνώοντ᾽ ὀλοοῖο φόβοιο 11.71; μνωόμενος προτέρης ἁρπακτύος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 95: _absolutely_, μνωομένῳ}} **when I remember him**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.106, compare 15.400}}; also, **turn one's mind** to a thing, φύγαδε μνώοντο ἕκαστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.697.}}
**II** **woo for one's bride, court** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), with _accusative_, μήτ᾽ αὐτὸν κτείνειν μήτε μνάασθαι ἄκοιτιν, of Aegisthus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.39; τὴν πάντες μνώοντο 11.287: with no _accusative_ expressed, 16.77, 19.529.}}
**II.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **sue for, solicit** a favour, office, etc., μνώμενος ἀρχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96}}; μνώμενον βασιληίην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 205; φιλοτιμίαν μνώμενοι ἢ στάσιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 210; εὔνοιαν ἑαυτῷ παρὰ τῶν στρατιωτῶν μ. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.9.11; πᾶσαν ἑαυτῷ πόλιν πατρίδα μ. 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 3.14.—_Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and late Prose; once in Comedy texts (see. above). (For signification 1 compare μιμνήσκ; in signification 11 perhaps from μνᾱ-}} = βνᾱ-, i. e. **g[uglide]nā**-, compare γυνή.)

3417:
** μνεία**, ἡ,
={μνήμη}, **remembrance**, βίου δὲ τοῦ παρόντος οὐ μνείαν ἔχεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 392, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 464, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 798b}}; κατά γε τὴν ἐμὴν μ. uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 6.1}}; μνείας χάριν, frequently in late epitaphs, {{Refs|“IG” 3.3112, +others}}
**II** **mention**, περί τινος μνείαν ποιεῖσθαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.100, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.160; περί τινος πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317e; τὴν μνείαν περί τινος ἀποδιδόναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 58b13; ὅ τι καὶ μνείας ἄξιον [prev. author] “Pol.” 1274b17; μ. τινῶν ποιεῖσθαι ἐπὶ τῶν προσευχῶν NT.Rom.1.2, +others, compare “epigram Gr.” 983.3 (1st c.BC)}}; **reminder**, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a}}; **commemoration**, αἱ μ. τῶν ἁγίων see reading in {{NT|NT.Rom.12.13.}}

3418:
** μνῆμα**,
_Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ **μνᾶμα**, ατος, τό, (μνάομαι, μιμνήσκω) **memorial, remembrance, record** of a person or thing, with _genitive_, μνῆμ᾽ Ἑλένης χειρῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.126; μ. ξείνοιο φίλοιο 21.40; τάφου μ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.619; μνᾶμα κακοζοΐας 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 120; μ. κάλλιστον ἀέθλων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.15 (of the crown of olive)}}; Νικοκλέος μνᾶμα κελαδῆσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 8(7).68; τῆς σῆς πορείας μ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 841}}; λυγρᾶς μνήματα Τροίας, of the sufferings of the Greeks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1210 (Lyric poetry)}}; μ... διὰ χειρὸς ἔχων, i. e. the dead body of his son, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1258 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** **mound** or **building** in honour of the dead, **monument, tomb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.167, epigram [prev. work] 228, “IG” 12.906, etc.; ἐν τοῖς δημοσίοις μνήμασιν κειμένους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.208}}; **coffin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1053.}}
**3** **memorial** dedicated to a god, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 138; μνάματα ναυ μαχίας [prev. author] 134, compare epigram cited in 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.14}}: generally, **monument**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 2 B (Sigeum, 6th c.BC).}}
**II** ={μνήμη}, **memory**, μ. ἔχουσ᾽ ἀγαθῶν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 112.}}

3419:
** μνημ-εῖον**,
_Doric dialect_ **μνᾱμεῖον**, _Ionic dialect_ **μνημήϊον**, τό, **memorial, remembrance, record** of a person or thing, μνημήϊα καταλιπέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 135; λόγων φερτάτων μ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.49, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 49, etc.}}; μνημεῖα ὅρκων **a record** of the oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1204; μνημεῖα κακῶν τε κἀγαθῶν ἀΐδια 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41}}; μνημεῖα τῆς δαπάνης **visible memorials**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321a40; ἐνομίζομεν τὰς συμφορὰς ἱκανὰ μ. τῇ πόλει καταλελεῖφθαι, ὥστε μηδ᾽ ἄν.. ἐπιθυμεῖν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 34.1}}; τὰ παίδων μαθήματα θαυμαστὸν ἔχει τι μ. the lessons of childhood cling strangely to the memory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26b}}; μνημεῖα καταλειφθῆναι τῶν μελλόντων ἔσεσθαι to be left behind **as reminders** of things to come, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 233a.}}
**2** of one dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 106 (_plural_); μνημεῖ᾽ Ὀρέστου.. προσθεῖναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 933}}; of an urn containing the ashes of the dead,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1126; τύμβον χῶσον κἀπίθες μνημεῖά μοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 702; τάφων τε καὶ τῶν ἄλλων μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414a}}; **tomb**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.13.6, NT.John.5.28, “SIG” 1234, etc.}}: generally, **monument**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120c (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.17, “IG” 14.1932 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

3420:
** μνήμ-η**,
_Doric dialect_ **μνάμα**, ἡ, (μνάομαι) **remembrance, memory** of a person or thing, _absolutely_ or with _genitive_, κακῶν μ. γίνεται οὐδεμία {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 798; οὔτ᾽ ἀγαθῶν μνήμην εἰδότες οὔτε κακῶν [prev. author] 1114; τῶν ἐμῶν μνάμα ποκ᾽ ἐσσεῖται πόνων 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 254}}; λείπεσθαι ἀθάνατον μ. (i.e. ἑαυτοῦ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.144; μ. ἔχειν τινός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1246, “OC” 509, etc.; τάφου μ. τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1585}}; οἱ ἄνθρωποι πρὸς ἃ ἔπασχον τὴν μ. ἐποιοῦντο made their **recollections** suit their sufferings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54}}; μνήμην πεποίηκεν has made [him] **remembered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414a6: _plural_, ἀγήρατοι μνῆμαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.79}}; of the dead, μακαρίας μνήμης, θείας μνήμης, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“IG” 7.175 (from Megara), 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 43 “Praef.”, etc.}}; κρατίστης μνήμης Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 26.30 (2nd c.AD)}}; μνήμης ἀρίστης {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 7.2808.6 (Hyettus, 3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **memory** as a power of the mind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 146, etc.; μνήμην ἁπάντων μουσομήτορ᾽ ἐργάνην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 461}}; proper to animals, opposed to ἀνάμνησις, of man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 34c, compare μνημονεύω 1.2}}; εἰπεῖν τι μνήμης ἄπο from **memory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1131; ἐν μνήμῃ λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26b; φυλάξαι τῇ μ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 783c}}; εἰς μ. ἀναληπτέον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 864b; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον μ. ἀνθρώπων ἐφικνεῖται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.8; φέρειν ἐν μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 435: _plural_, αἱ πολλαὶ μ. τοῦ αὐτοῦ πράγματος μιᾶς ἐμπειρίας δύναμιν ἀποτελοῦσιν}} all the **memories, acts of memory**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 980b29, compare “APo.” 100a5}}; **powers of memory**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1362b24 (assuming variant).}}
**3** **memorial, record**, κυπαρίττιναι μνῆμαι εἰς τὸν ἔπειτα χρόνον καταγεγραμμέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741c}}; μνῆμαι ἐν μέτροις καὶ ἄνευ μέτρων **inscriptions**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a34}}; μ. μυθολόγος mythological **record, history**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.55, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ αἱρέσεων τοῖς εἰσαγομένοις” 2.}}
**II** **mention, notice** of a thing, μ. ποιεῖσθαί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.15, etc.}}; μ. ποιήσασθαι περί or ὑπέρ τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.7.12, 2.71.1; ἡ ὑπὲρ τῶν δικαίων μ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.52; μ. ἔχειν τινός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, etc. (compare above 1); μ. ἐπασκέειν [prev. author] 2.77.}}
**III** μνήμη βασίλειος the imperial **cabinet** or **archives**, τῆς β. μ. προεστώς, = Latin _a memoria_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.8.4, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 76.14 (probably).}}
**IV** ={μνῆμα}, **tomb**, {{Refs|“AJP” 48.18 (from Rome).}}

3421:
** μνημον-εύω**,
_perfect_ ἐμνημόνευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 103b (ἀπ-), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.1; μεμνημόνευκα 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.17.2}}:—_passive_, _future_ μνημονευθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.128}}; also μνημονεύσομαι in _passive_ sense (see. infr. B): _aorist_ ἐμνημονεύθην{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]192: _perfect_ ἐμνημόνευμαι (δι-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117e}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐμνημονευσάμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.50}}: (μνήμων):— **call to mind, remember, think of**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 783, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 121, “fragment” 1120.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.55U., etc.: with _genitive_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 94, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 1, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, **remember** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 264}}; μ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 480a}}; εἰ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.11}}; ὡς.. {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.10.6.}}
**2** _absolutely_, μ. γὰρ καλῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 122, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 499e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 142}}; opposed to ἀναμιμνήσκεσθαι, as animal instinct from human faculty, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Memoria” 453a6.}}
**II** **call to** another's **mind, mention, say**, with _accusative_, ἀληθῆ -εύεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 646b, etc.}}; μ. τινί τινος **make mention of** a thing **to** another, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 2}}: with _genitive_, **make mention of**, τοῦ ἔθνους ἐν ταῖς ἱστορίαις J.[same place],{{LXX+1st c.AD+|compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 32, etc.; μ. ἀδελφῶν LXX.1Mac.12.11.}}
**III** **serve as** μνήμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.3, “SIG” 45.11 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; μ. ἁγναῖς θεαῖς {{Refs|“IG” 14.204 (from Acrae), compare 9(1).689 (Corc.).}}
**B** _passive_, **to be remembered, had in memory**, μνημονεύσεται χάρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 334, compare “OGI” 666.22 (1st c.AD)}}; τὰ ἐκ τοῦ πρὶν χρόνου μνημονευόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23; τὴν δόξαν τὴν εἰς ἅπαντα τὸν χρόνον μνημονευθησομένην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.128; τὸν ἅπαντα χρόνον μνημονευθήσεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231; οἱ μνημονευόμενοι ἄνθρωποι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.2}}: with _infinitive_, ἐμνημονεύετο γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47}}: with _participle_, πόλεμος.. εὖ πολεμηθεὶς μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600a.}}

3422:
** μνημόσῠνον**, τό,
**remembrance, memorial** of a thing, μνημόσυνον ἑωυτοῦ λιπέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.185, 4.166; μνημόσυνον λιπέσθαι [prev. author] 1.186, 2.101; μνημόσυνα ἀποδέξασθαι [prev. work] 148, +others}}: rare in early _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.11; μ. στοργῆς “Anthologia Graeca” 12.68 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); εἰς μ. τινός NT.Matt.26.13, cf. NT.Act.10.4.}}
**2** **memorandum, reminder**, μνημόσυνα γράψομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 538; τουτὶ.. ἔστω τὸ μ. μοι [prev. work] 559.}}
**3** **mark, scar**, μ. ὑποκαταλιπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.20.}}

3423:
** μνηστ-εύω**,
_Doric dialect_ **μναστ-**, _aorist_ ἐμνήστευσα: _perfect_ μεμνήστευκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.23}}:—_middle_, _future_ μνηστεύσομαι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 29}}: _perfect_ μεμνήστευμαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista (see. below)}}, in _passive_ sense, [{{NT|NT.Luke.1.27, 2.5 (variant{ἐμν}-)}}]:—**court, seek in marriage**, with _accusative_, ἀγαθήν τε γυναῖκα καὶ ἀφνειοῖο θύγατρα μνηστεύειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.277; τὴν πλεῖστοι.. μνήστευον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 33.2; ἐμνήστευσε τὴν γυναῖκα λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.37; μ. γάμους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 847, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773b}}; **woo and win, espouse**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1112, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.6}}:—_middle_, **court for oneself**, Περσεφόνης γάμον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.5.12, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.8.23, 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus [same place]: censured by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 9, but used by him, “Merc.Cond.” 23, “Tox.” 37; ὁ τὴν κόρην μεμνηστευμένος [prev. author] “Asin.” 26; - ευσαμένου μου τῷ.. υἱῷ.. τὴν.. θυγατέρα “PFlor.” 36.4 (4th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, of the woman, μναστευθείσᾳ᾽ ξ Ἑλλάνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 208 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.39.}}
**II** **promise in marriage, betroth**, τινά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 313}}; γάμον μνηστεύειν (with or without τινι) **to bring about** a marriage **for** another, help him to a wife, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 265, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.511:}} —_passive_, τῇ μεμνηστευμένῃ αὐτῷ γυναικί his **betrothed** wife, {{NT|NT.Luke.2.5.}}
**II.b** _metaphorically_, τοσόνδε κίνδυνον αὐτῷ ὁ ὄλεθρος τῶν ἀδελφῶν μ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.1.1; ἡδονήν [τινι] μ. τῷ κάλλει 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 180; ἡμῖν τοῦ μέλους τὸ ἐνδόσιμον μ. [prev. work] 186.}}
**III** generally, **sue, canvass for** a thing, with _accusative_, χειροτονίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.15}}: with _infinitive_, μνηστευόμενος ἄρχειν ἑκόντων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 58:}} —also in _middle_, μ. πόλεμον, διαλλαγάς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.17.3, “AJ” 5.7.4.}}
**IV** λόγον μ. τινί **pass off** a speech upon a person, **hawk** it to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Socratis et Socraticorum Epistulae “Ep.” 30.13.}}

3426:
** μόδιος**, ὁ,
= Latin _modius_, a dry measure, = 1/6 μέδιμνος, for which it is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.43, compare “OGI” 533.30 (1st c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 33.}}
**2** **vessel of this capacity,** {{NT|NT.Matt.5.15.}}
**II** a measure of length, = 200 ὀργυιαί, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Geometry texts” 4.12.}}

3427:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

3428:
** μοιχ-ᾰλίς**, ίδος
(also _accusative_ μοιχαλίν [{{LXX+NT+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Hos.3.1), ἡ, ={μοιχάς}, NT.Rom.7.3, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.9, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(1).264, etc.}}]; in religious sense, **unfaithful to God,** {{NT|NT.Jam.4.4}}: so as _adjective_, **adulterous**, γενεά {{NT|NT.Matt.12.39, etc.}}
**II** ={μοιχεία}, {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.14.}}

3429:
** μοιχ-άω**,
={μοιχεύω} 1: _metaphorically_, μοιχᾶν τὴν θάλατταν **have dalliance with** the sea, applied by Callicratidas to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Conon Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.15}}:— _passive_, **commit adultery**, of a man, {{NT|NT.Matt.5.32}}; of a woman, {{NT|NT.Mark.10.12}}: _metaphorically_, **to be unfaithful to God**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.3.8, LXX.Eze.23.37.}}
**2** **falsify**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.39.}}

3431:
** μοιχ-εύω**,
**commit adultery with** a woman, **debauch** her, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 558, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 360b}}:—_passive_, of the woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 980 (_anapaest meter_); μοιχευθῆναί τινι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 586a3; μεμοιχεῦσθαι ὑπ᾽ ἀλλήλων, of birds, [prev. work] 619a10.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **worship idolatrously**, τὸ ξύλον καὶ τὸν λίθον {{LXX|LXX.Jer.3.9.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **commit adultery**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 11.3; ἐμοίχευσάς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1076, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129b21.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, in _future_ _middle_ (in _passive_ sense), οὐ μοιχεύσεταί μου τὰ φιλήματα her kisses **shall** not **be adulterously stolen** from me, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 4.8.}}

3432:
** μοιχός**, ὁ,
**adulterer, paramour**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 74, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” [1127.6], 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 168, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191d, etc.: proverbial, θύραν, δι᾽ ἧς γαλῆ καὶ μ. οὐκ εἰσέρχεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 6; ὅρκοι μοιχῶν 5th c.BC: Philonides Comicus 7}}; κεκαρμένος μοιχὸν μιᾷ μαχαίρᾳ having the head close shaven with a razor, as was done by way of punishment to persons taken in adultery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 849.}}
**II** generally, **paramour**, of a sodomite, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1160.26 (3rd-4th c.AD). (Cf. ὀμείχω.)}}
**III** **idolatrous person,** {{NT|NT.Jam.4.4.}}

3435:
** μολύνω**,
_future_ - ῠνῶ {{LXX|LXX.Cant.5.3:}}_aorist_ ἐμόλῡνα{{LXX|LXX.Gen.37.31}}: _perfect_ μεμόλυγκα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.68}}:—_passive_, _future_ μολυνθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Zech.14.2:}}_aorist 1_ ἐμολύνθην{{LXX|LXX.Sir.22.13:}}_perfect_ μεμόλυσμαι{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Esd.8.83, 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 33, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.6.1, μεμόλυμμαι LXX.Isa.65.4, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.186}}:—**stain, sully, defile**, τὴν ὑπήνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1286; ἑαυτοὺς τῷ πηλῷ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 571b18, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.10}}; simply, **sprinkle**, ἀλεύρῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus Comedy texts 1.24}}; **make a beast of**, τινας (of Circe) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 310}}; **defile, debauch**, παῖδα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.87: _metaphorically_, χεῖρας ἁρπαγῇ 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 47}}:—_passive_, **become vile, disgrace oneself**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.81; μετὰ γυναικῶν NT.Rev.14.4}}; ὥσπερ θηρίον ὕειον ἐν ἀμαθίᾳ μολύνεσθαι **wallow** in ignorance, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 535e; ὁ μολυνόμενος ὑπὸ τοῦ ὄψου 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 18b p.101 H.; ἡ συνείδησις αὐτῶν μολύνεται NT.1Cor.8.7.}}
**2** **stain, dye**, χιτωνίσκον αἵματι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.3.4:}} — _passive_, ἔρια μεμολυσμένα ἄνθεσι{{Refs|[prev. work] 3.6.1.}}
**II** see at {μωλύω}.

3438:
** μονή**, ἡ,
(μένω) **abiding, tarrying**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1129, “HF” 957, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 418 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.5, etc.}}; opposed to ἔξοδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94}}; opposed to φορά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 437b}}; opposed to κίνησις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 205a17}}; τὴν μονὴν ποιεῖσθαι make **delay, tarry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.131; μὴ λαμβάνειν μονὴν μηδὲ στάσιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.41.4; μ. τις καὶ στάσις τῆς φωνῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.12 M.: _plural_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 230a20; κινήσεις ἢ μοναί [prev. author] “de An.” 408b18; μονὰς ποιεῖσθαι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.12}}; **persistence, continuance**, τοῦ αἰσθήματος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 99b36.}}
**2** **permanence**, τῆς γῆς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.10, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 36.}}
**3** Grammars, **preservation**, τοῦ ν ¯ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50.25.}}
**II** **stopping-place, station**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.31.7}}; **apartment,** {{NT|NT.John.14.2,23}}; **quarters, billets**, {{Refs|“OGI” 527.5 (from Hierapolis).}}
**II.2** **monastery**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 133.1, etc.}}
**III** **appearance** in a court of law, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 93.2, 111.31 (3rd c.BC).}}

3439:
** μονο-γενής**, ές,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **μουνο-**, (γένος) **the only member of a kin** or **kind**: hence, generally, **only, single**, παῖς {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 376, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.221, cf. NT.John.1.14, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 32.1}}; of Hecate, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 426.}}
**2** **unique**, of τὸ ὄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.4; εἷς ὅδε μ. οὐρανὸς γεγονώς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31b, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 22; θεὸς ὁ μ. “Sammelb.” 4324.15.}}
**3** μ. αἷμα **one and the same** blood, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1685.}}
**4** Grammars, **having one form for all genders**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 145.18.}}
**5** name of the foot ¯ ¯ ¯ ?~X, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 3.3.}}
**II** _adverb_ -νῶς, φέρεται μ. ἐν ἑνὶ τόπῳ grows **only** in one place, {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 56, compare 11.}}
**II.2** **in a unique manner**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.13,14.}}

3440:
Related to: ** μονόω**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **μουνόω**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.117, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}; but **μον-** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.470}}: (μόνος):— **make single** or **solitary**, ἡμετέρην γενεὴν μούνωσε Κρονίων **made** our race **single**, i. e. **allowed but one son** in each generation, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.117}}; μ. τὸν Φίλιππον **leave** him **isolated**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.16.10}}; **get alone**, τινὰ ἐν σπήλυγγι {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.451}}; **strip of predicates, make unique**, [θεόν] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.8.15.}}
**II** more frequently in _passive_, **to be left alone, forsaken**, ἐνὶ Τρώεσσι μονωθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]; μουνωθέντα παρ᾽ οἴεσιν ἢ παρὰ βουσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.386}}; ἐμουνοῦντο **they were left each man by himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.123}}; μουνωθέντα **taken apart, without witnesses** (variant for{μουνόθεν}), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.116; γυνὴ μονωθεῖσ᾽ οὐδέν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 749}}; of animals when hunted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.9}}; when **left solitary**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 578b33}}; of the soul, **to be separated** from the body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 36}}; of things, **to be taken alone**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096b17}}; **to be isolated** in thought, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 195.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _person_, μεμουνωμένοι συμμάχων **deserted** by allies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.102, compare 6.15, 7.139}}; μονωθεὶς δάμαρτος, σοῦ μονούμενος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 296, 380; δεσποτῶν μονούμενος [prev. author] “Rh.” 871; μονωθεῖσ᾽ ἀπὸ πατρός [prev. author] “IA” 669; μονωθεὶς μετ᾽ ὀλίγων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101: _absolutely_, μεμονωμένων εἰ κρατήσειαν [prev. author] 2.81, compare 5.40, 58. b. with _genitive_ _of things_, μεμονωμένοι τῆς τῶν ἱππέων βοηθείας}} **bereft of**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.43}}; μονούμενος τῶν ἀγαθῶν **separated from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 710b}}; μονωθεῖσαι φρονήσεως **without**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 46e}}; μονωθεὶς ἐκ τῆς εἱρκτῆς, i. e. **set free** from.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ax.” 370d.}}

3441:
** μόνος**, η, ον,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **μοῦνος**, the only form used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (as in all derivations except μονόω), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, also by 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.27, “I.” 5(4).12, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.80, +others, by 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} both in _iambic_ and Lyric poetry, by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus only in _compound_ μουνώψ, by 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus only in μούναρχος}}: _Doric dialect_ **μῶνος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.64, 20.45}}:— **alone, solitary**, μοῦνος ἐὼν πολέσιν μετὰ Καδμείοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.388; ἢ ὅ γε μοῦνος ἐών 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.217; μούνω ἄνευθ᾽ ἄλλων 16.239: joined with ἐρῆμος, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 887, “Ph.” 470; μόνοι γὰρ ἐσμέν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 21; ἄνθρωπος πρεσβύτης καὶ μ. “BGU” 180.23 (2nd c.AD)}}; φυγὴ μόνου πρὸς μόνον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.11.}}
**2** with _genitive_, σοῦ μόνος **bereft of** thee, **without** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 511; also μοῦνος ἀπ᾽ ἄλλων “hymnus ad Mercurium” 193, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 183 (Lyric poetry); ἑτάρων ἄπο μ. 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.908.}}
**II** **only**, μοῦνον Λαέρτην Ἀρκείσιος υἱὸν ἔτικτε, μοῦνον δ᾽ αὖτ᾽ Ὀδυσῆα πατὴρ τέκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.118, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.482; μόνης γὰρ σοῦ κλύων ἀνέξεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 838, compare 632 (_anapaest meter_), “Pr.” 425 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; χοίνικος μόνας ἁλῶν for a gallon of salt **only**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 814}}; **single**, οὐκ ἄρα μοῦνον ἔην Ἐρίδων γένος, ἀλλὰ.. δύω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 11, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1280}}; εἷς μοῦνος or μόνος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.38, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 63: once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, μία μούνη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.227: joined with αὐτός, αὐτὼ μόνω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211c; αὐτοὶ καθ᾽ αὑτοὺς μόνοι [prev. author] “Plt.” 307e.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, μοῦνος ποταμῶν **alone** of rivers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25, compare 29; μ. θεῶν γὰρ Θάνατος οὐ δώρων ἐρᾷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 161; μ. τῶν ἄλλων ποιητῶν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 102}}; but μοῦνος πάντων ἀνθρώπων he **and no other** of all men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.25}}; ἀνδρῶν γε μοῦνος he **and no other**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1250, compare “El.” 531; ὦ μόνα ὦ φίλα γυναικῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 460 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** frequently repeated in the same clause, ξυμπεσὼν μόνος μόνοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 467}}; Ἕκτορος μόνος μόνου.. ἐναντίος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1283; σὺν τέκνοις μόνη μόνοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 513; μόνος μόνῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.137.}}
**II.4** expressing rhetorically pre-eminence in an action or quality, μόνα κατέχεσθαι ποιεῖ are **unique** for causing possession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215c, compare 222a, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 261, “OT” 299, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.57; [ἐπέδειξε] σαφέστατα μόνος ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.9.}}
**III** _superlative_ μονώτατος **one above all others**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 352, “Pl.” 182, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 89, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.137, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.350 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**IV** **made in one piece**, τάπης {{Refs|“Edict.Diocl.” 19.23.}}
**B** _adverb_ **μόνως**, **on one condition only**, followed by εἰ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.81, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.5, “Cyr.” 3.2.23}}; **in one way only**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “in Aristotelis Physica paraphrasis - pars” 29.22, +others}}; **in a unique manner**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 98}}: later, simply, **only**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.53 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.559, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.254 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 4.7, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.479 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**B.II** _neuter_ as _adverb_, μόνον **alone, only**, οὐχ ἅπαξ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 211, etc.: frequently with _imperative_, μ. φύλαξαι [prev. author] “Supp.” 1012; ἀποκρίνου μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494d; so μ. Κράτος συγγένοιτό σοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 244; μὴ 'μὲ καταπίῃς μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 219, etc.}}; ἐὰν μ. if **only**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a3}}; οὐσίαν.., οὐ χωριστὴν μ. **only** not separable, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1025b28.}}
**B.II.2** οὐ μόνον.., ἀλλὰ καὶ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1282, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.17, etc.}}; οὐ μ., ἀλλὰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 555: μόνον}} is sometimes omitted, μὴ τοὺς ἐγγύς, ἀλλὰ καὶ τοὺς ἄπωθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359, “Ph.” 1480 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.II.3** μόνον οὐ **all but, well nigh**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 516, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.220, etc.; μόνον οὐκ ἐπὶ ταῖς κεφαλαῖς περιφέρουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600d: in codices frequently written μονονού, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.109.2, etc.; μονονουχί 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.2, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.102.4.}}
**B.III** κατὰ μόνας **alone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 37, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.38, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281b34, etc.}}
**B.IV** μόνῃ, ={μόνον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.583d codices (Prob.from *μόνϝος.)}}

