3444:
** μορφ-ή**, ἡ,
**form, shape**, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (not in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus), σοὶ δ᾽ ἔπι μὲν μορφὴ ἐπέων}} thou hast **comeliness** of words, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.367 (compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis (at [prev. work]))}}; so probably ἄλλος μὲν.. εἶδος ἀκιδνότερος πέλει ἀνήρ, ἀλλὰ θεὸς μορφὴν ἔπεσι στέφει God adds a crown of **shapeliness** to his words, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.170: frequently later, μορφὰς δύο ὀνομάζειν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.53; μορφὴν ἀλλάξαντα 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 137.1; μορφὰν βραχύς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).53}}; μορφῆς μέτρα **shape** and size, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1063: periphrastic, μορφῆς φύσις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 496}}; μορφῆς σχῆμα, τύπωμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 992, “Ph.” 162; τὴν αὐτὴν τοῦ σχήματος μορφήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 640b34; καὶ Γαῖα, πολλῶν ὀνομάτων μ. μία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 212}}; ὀνειράτων ἀλίγκιοι μορφαῖσιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 449; νυκτέρων φαντασμάτων ἔχουσι μορφάς [prev. author] “fragment” 312; προὔπεμψεν ἀντὶ φιλτάτης μ. σποδόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1159}}; of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.1.12 (_plural_)}}; especially with _reference_ to **beauty of form**, ὑπέρφατον μορφᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.65}}; οἷς ποτιστάξῃ χάρις εὐκλέα μ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.76, compare “IG” 42 (1).121.119 (Epid., 4th c.BC), LXX.Tob.1.13, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 1.6, etc.; σῶμα μορφῆς ἐμῆς “OGI” 383.41 (Commagene, 1st c.BC); μορφῆς εἰκόνας [prev. work]27; χαρακτῆρα μορφῆς ἐμῆς [prev. work]60.}}
**2** generally, **form, fashion, appearance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 78, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 699, “El.” 199 (Lyric poetry)}}; **outward form**, opposed to εἶδος, ἑκατέρω τῶ εἴδεος πολλαὶ μ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 5; ἀλλάττοντα τὸ αὑτοῦ εἶδος εἰς πολλὰς μορφάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 380d; μ. θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.13, cf. NT.Phil.2.6, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 304; ἡρώων εἴδεα καὶ μορφάς 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1193}}; κατά τε μορφὰς καὶ φωνάς **gesticulations** and cries, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 14.9}}; τὴν μ. μελάγχρους, τῇ μ. μελίχροας, in **complexion**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 143, 144.}}
**3** **kind, sort**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 382, 1068 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397c, etc.}} (Possibly _cognate_ with Latin _forma_ for **morg[uglide]hmā**, with **f** by dissimilation, compare μύρμηξ.)

3446:
** μόρφ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**shaping, bringing into shape**, σχηματισμὸς καὶ μ. τῶν δένδρων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.7.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.640, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 27, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus Astr. 1.3.}}
**II** **form, semblance,** {{NT|NT.Rom.2.20, NT.2Tim.3.5.}}

3448:
** μόσχος**, ὁ, ἡ,
**B** **calf, young bull**, which form the god Apis was believed to assume, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.28, compare 2.41, “PCair.Zen.” 78.6 (3rd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 6279.7 (3rd c.BC)}}: as _feminine_, **heifer, young cow**, μόσχους ἀμέλγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 389, compare “Ba.” 736}}; πεζαὶ μ., ={ἑταῖραι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 169}}; ᾄδειν ἐπὶ μόσχῳ for the prize of a **calf** (nisi to be read ἐπὶ Μόσχῳ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 13.}}
**B.2** _metaphorically_, **boy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1623}}: as _feminine_, **girl, maid**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 526, “Andr.” 711, etc.}}
**B.3** **any young animal**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 163, “Ba.” 1185 (both Lyric poetry)}}; even of birds, μ. χελιδόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 47.}}
**B.4** μ. θαλάσσιος **seal,** {{Refs|Edict.Diocl. 8.37.}}

3450:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

3451:
** μουσῐκός**, ή, όν,
_Doric dialect_ **μωσικός**, ά, όν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Theages Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.118}}:— **musical**, ἀγῶνες μ. καὶ γυμνικοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1163, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104; χοροί τε καὶ ἀγῶνες μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 828c}}; τὰ μουσικά **music**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.38, “Sammelb.” 6319.54 (1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus), “SIG” 578.18 (Teos, 2nd c.BC)}}. _adverb_ - κῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.108d, etc.}}; compare {μουσική}
**II** of persons, **skilled in music, musical**, X.[same place], etc.; ποιητικοὶ καὶ μ. ἄνδρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802b; κύκνος καὶ ἄλλα ζῷα μ. [prev. author] “R.” 620a; περὶ αὐλοὺς -ώτατοι 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.176e}}; **lyric poet**, opposed to epic, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243a (but opposed to μελοποιός, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.96 K.)}}; μ., οἱ, professional **musicians**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 383.162 (Commagene, 1st c.BC), “PFlor.” 74.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; μουσικὸς καὶ μελῶν ποητής {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 662.6 (Delos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** generally, **votary of the Muses, man of letters and accomplishments, scholar**, opposed to ἀμαθής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 191; ἀνὴρ σοφὸς καὶ μ. [prev. author] “V.” 1244; ἀνδρὸς φιλοσόφου ἢ φιλοκάλου ἢ μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248d, +others}}; πόλις -ωτάτη **most full of liberal arts**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 8.4; ἡ τῶν νέων οὐσία μουσικωτάτη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729a: with}}_infinitive_, παρ᾽ ὄχλῳ -ώτεροι λέγειν **more accomplished in** speaking before a mob, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 989.}}
**III** of things, **elegant, delicate**, βρώματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Dioxippus Comicus 1; ἥδιον οὐδέν, οὐδὲ -ώτερον 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 23}}; **harmonious, fitting**, τροφὴ μέση καὶ μ., τὸν Δώριον τρόπον τῆς τύχης ὡς ἀληθῶς ἡρμοσμένη {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “Vita Isidori” 50.}} _adverb_ -κῶς **harmoniously, suitably**, οἱ λόγοι οὐ πάνυ μ. λέγονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333a; μ. ἐρᾶν [prev. author] “R.” 403a; ὀρθῶς καὶ μ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 816b; εὐρύθμως καὶ μ. εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.16}}; μ. ἅλας δοῦναι, ὄψον σκευάσαι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 11.10, 3rd c.BC: Nicomachus Comicus 1.9}}: _comparative_ -ωτέρως, λέγειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395b29}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 873.}}

3452:
** μυελός**, ὁ,
**marrow**, μυελὸς αὖτε σφονδυλίων ἔκπαλθ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.482, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73bf, 91b, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.2.6, etc.: _plural_, 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100b}}; **brain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 781, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 8.4}}; μ. ῥαχίτης spinal **cord**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 499; μ. νωτιαῖος Diocl.fragment 141.}}
**2** **fat**, χηνὸς μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 109, +others}}
**3** **marrow** as good food, ἐπὶ γούνασι πατρὸς μυελὸν οἶον ἔδεσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.501 (but ἄλφιτα μ. ἀνδρῶν}}, as becoming or making **marrow**, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.290, compare 20.108): _metaphorically_, φάγεσθε τὸν μ. τῆς γῆς LXX.Gen.45.18.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, νεαρὸς μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 76 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πρὸς ἄκρον μ. ψυχῆς **marrow, inmost part**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 255 (_anapaest meter_)}}; Τρινακρίας μ., of Syracuse, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.18.}}
**5** generally, **soft, marrow-like meat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 186.10. [ῡ always in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}: ῠ always in _Attic dialect_; so also in the derivation words.]

3453:
** μῠέω**,
(μύω, which see) **initiate into the mysteries**, μυῶν.. ἄλλους ξένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.132, compare “IG” 12.6.113; ἐκ τοῦ μυεῖν καὶ ἐποπτεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 333e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.21; ἐμύησε καὶ μυεῖ τοὺς Ἕλληνας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.607b: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ξένους ἐμύει θεούς 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.37}}:—more frequently in _passive_, **to be initiated**, ὁ βουλόμενος μυεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65; ὅσοι μεμυήμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 456}}; οἱ μεμυημένοι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 158, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.28; δεῖ γὰρ μυηθῆναί με πρὶν τεθνηκέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 375}}; μυηθῆναι ἀφ᾽ ἑστίας, see at {ἑστί; τοῦ ἀφ᾽ ἑστίας μυουμένου} probably in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 12.6.108; μυηθεῖσαν ἀφ᾽ ἑστίας “SIG” 853 (Eleusis, 1st c.AD)}}; θεοπρόποι ἦλθον.., οἵτινες μυηθέντες ἐνεβάτευσαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 530.15 (Notium, 2nd c.AD)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, **to be initiated in** a thing, τὰ μυστήρια μυεῦνται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 14}}; ὅστις τὰ Καβείρων ὄργια μεμύηται in the mysteries of the Cabiri, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51}}; τὰ μεγάλα (i.e. μυστήρια) μεμύησαι, πρὶν τὰ σμικρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497c; τὰ ἐρωτικὰ μυηθῆναι [prev. author] “Smp.” 209e, compare “Phdr.” 250c; τὰ λεοντικὰ μ. 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 15: also with _dative_, ἐμυήθην θεοῖς Theophilus Comicus 1.4; μυεῖσθαι γάμῳ 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.4.}}
**II** generally, **teach, instruct**, with _infinitive_, ἐμύησάς τινα ἰδεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.385 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus); ἀλλά μ᾽ ἀνὴρ ἐμύησ᾽ Ἑλικωνίδα (i.e. εἶναι) [prev. work] 9.162}}:—_passive_, μεμύημαι καὶ χορτάζεσθαι καὶ πεινᾶν {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Phil.4.12; κυβερνᾶν μυηθήσομαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4.21.}}

3454:
** μῦθος**, ὁ,
**word, speech**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and other Poets, in _singular_ and _plural_, ἔπος καὶ μῦθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.561; opposed to ἔργον, μύθων τε ῥητῆρ᾽ ἔμεναι πρηκτῆρά τε ἔργων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.443, compare 19.242}}; especially **mere word**, μύθοισιν, opposed to ἔγχεϊ,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|18.252; ἔργῳ κοὐκέτι μύθῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1080 (_anapaest meter_), etc.:}} —in special relations:
**2** **public speech**, μ. ἄνδρεσσι μελήσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.358; μύθοισιν σκολιοῖς 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 194}}; μύθου ἐπισχεσίη the submission of a **plea**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.71; πρὶν ἂν ἀμφοῖν μ. ἀκούσῃς, οὐκ ἂν δικάσαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 725}}; μύθοισι κεκάσθαι to be skilled in **speech**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.157.}}
**3** **conversation**, mostly in _plural_, {{Refs|4.214, 239, etc.}}
**4** **thing said, fact, matter**, μῦθον δέ τοι οὐκ ἐπικεύσω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 744; τὸν ὄντα μ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 346}}; **threat, command**, ἠπείλησεν μῦθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.388, compare 25, 16.83}}; **charge, mission**, {{Refs|9.625}}; **counsel, advice**, {{Refs|7.358.}}
**5** **thing thought, unspoken word, purpose, design**, 1.545 (_plural_); μύθων οὓς μνηστῆρες ἐνὶ φρεσὶ βυσσοδόμευον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.676, compare 777; ἔχετ᾽ ἐν φρεσὶ μῦθον 15.445; ἔχε σιγῇ μ., ἐπίτρεψον δὲ θεοῖσι 19.502, compare 11.442}}; **matter**, θεοῖσι μῦθον ἐπιτρέψαι {{Refs|22.289}}; μῦθον μυθείσθην, τοῦ εἵνεκα λαὸν ἄγειραν the **reason** why.., {{Refs|3.140.}}
**6** **saying**, κατὰ τὸν ἡμέτερον μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 980a}}; οὐκ ἐμὸς ὁ μ. ἀλλ᾽.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 484, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177a, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 56, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.601, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.661a}}; **saw, proverb**, τριγέρων μ. τάδε φωνεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 314 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**7** **talk** of men, **rumour**, ἀγγελίαν.. τὰν ὁ μέγας μ. ἀέξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 226 (Lyric poetry), compare 188 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 72}}; **report, message**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 67 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1340.}}
**II** **tale, story, narrative**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.94, 4.324, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 11, etc.: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus like the later λόγος, without distinction of true or false, μ. παιδός}} **of** or **about** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.492: so in Trag., ἀκούσει μῦθον ἐν βραχεῖ λόγῳ (χρόνῳ codex M.) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 713; μύθων τῶν Λιβυστικῶν [prev. author] “fragment” 139.1: in Prose, τὸν εἰκότα μ.}} the like ly **story**, like lihood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 29d: proverbial, μ. ἀπώλετο}}, either of a **story** which never comes to an end, or of one told to those who do not listen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 59, 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164d, compare “R.” 621b, “Lg.” 645b, “Phlb.” 14a}}; μ. ἐσώθη 'that's the end of the **story**', {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**II.2** **fiction** (opposed to λόγος, **historic truth**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.29 (_plural_), “N.” 7.23 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, “Prt.” 320c, 324d, etc.}}
**II.3** generally, **fiction**, μ. ἴδιοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.5}}; **legend, myth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330d, “Lg.” 636c, etc.; ὁ περὶ θεῶν μ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.65U.; τοὺς μ. τοὺς ἐπιχωρίους γέγραφεν “SIG” 382.7 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4** **professed work of fiction, children's story, fable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 377a}}; of Aesop's **fables**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 356b11.}}
**II.5** **plot** of a comedy or tragedy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1449b5, 1450a4, 1451a16.}}
**III** ={στάσις}, {{Refs|Panyasis Epicus in “Coll.Alex.” p.249, variant in Batrachomyomachia 135}}; compare μυθιήτης.

3455:
** μῡκάομαι**,
_future_ -ήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.730 (Demetrius Astrologus Bith.), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Phal.” 1.11}}: _aorist_ ἐμῡκησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 292, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.37: used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} once in _present_ _participle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.413}}, elsewhere in _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ μύκον [ῠ], _Epic dialect_ _perfect_ μέμῡκα (also in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 352 (Lyric poetry))}}: _pluperfect_ ἐμεμύκειν or μεμύκειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.395: _Epic dialect_ iterative μύκεσκε only in “Etymologicum Magnum” 624.40}}:—properly of oxen, **low, bellow**, ὁ δὲ μακρὰ μεμυκώς [ὁ ταῦρος] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.580; μεμυκὼς ἠΰτε ταῦρος 21.237; πόριες.. μυκώμεναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.413, cf. A.[same place], 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 738}}; of a calf, Theoc. [prev. cited]; of Heracles in agony, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 870 (troch.: so comically, ἔβλεψεν δριμὺ κἀμυκᾶτο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 562); οἷον μυκτὴρ μυκᾶται [prev. author] “V.” 1488; μάτηρ κεφαλὰν μυκήσατο παιδὸς ἑλοῖσα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 26.20; ὥσπερ λέων μ. NT.Rev.10.3.}}
**2** of things, πύλαι μύκον οὐρανοῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.749; μέγα δ᾽ ἀμφὶ πύλαι μύκον 12.460}}; of a shield, μέγα δ᾽ ἀμφὶ σάκος μύκε δουρὸς ἀκωκῇ **rang**, {{Refs|20.260}}; of meat, ἀμφ᾽ ὀβελοῖσι μεμύκει **bellowed** upon the spits (a portent), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.395; μέμυκε δὲ γαῖα καὶ ὕλη 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 508; βροντῆς μυκησαμένης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 292; κόχλον ἑλὼν μυκήσατο κοῖλον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.75:—rare in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396b, 615e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 368a25.}}—An _aorist_ _active_, [τυμπάνου] βαρὺ μυκήσαντος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.220.11 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus). (Onomatopoeic word.)}}

3456:
** μυκτηρ-ίζω**,
**turn up the nose, sneer at**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 323 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, LXX.Pro.1.30, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.217}}:—_passive_, **to be mocked**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.20.7}}: hence, **to be outwitted,** {{NT|NT.Gal.6.7.}}
**II** **bleed at the nose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.123.}}

3457:
** μῠλ-ικός**, ή, όν,
(μύλη {{Refs|1)}} **for a mill**, λίθος {{NT|NT.Luke.17.2.}}
**II** (μύλη V) **of** or **for the grinders**, ἡ μ. (i.e. ἔμπλαστρος) remedy **for toothache**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.869, 877.}}

3458:
** μύλος** [ῠ], ὁ,
={μύλη}, **mill**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.11.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.549d, 830d}}; μ. καβαλλαρικὸς ἐν λίθοις, μ. ὀνικός, μ. ὑδραλετικός, {{Refs|“Edict.Diocl.” 15.52,53, 54.}}
**2** **millstone, PCair. Zen.**355.84 (uncertain, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC), “Anthologia Graeca” 11.253 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); γυνὴ ἔρριψε κλάσμα μύλου LXX.2Ki.11.21; μ. ὀνικός En. NT.Matt.18.6, NT.Mark.9.42; μύλους σφυρηλάτους ἀργυροῦς 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.1.13: _metaphorically_, ὀψὲ θεῶν ἀλέουσι μύλοι, ἀλέουσι δὲ λεπτά “App.Prov.” 4.48}}: generally, **stone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 241.}}
**3** **grinder, molar**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.31.}}
**II** _poetry_ for μύλλος (which see), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.130.}}
**III** ={μύλη} IV, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.36, 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus}} see at {ἐμυλώθη}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

3461:
** μῡρῐ-άς**, δος, ἡ,
_Attic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ μυριαδῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 1.428}}:—**number of** {{Refs|10, 000}}**myriad**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 91, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 3.2, etc.; μ. πρώτη 10, 000 and μ. δευτέρα 10, 000}} **times** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|10, 000, 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 5.8}}: frequently of **countless numbers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 830 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μυριάδες ἐτῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 93}}; of money (i.e. δραχμῶν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 829 (_anapaest meter_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 44}}; of corn (i.e. μεδίμνων), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.91, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.32}}; also (i.e. ἀρταβῶν), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1259.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** as _adjective_, φύστις μ. ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 927 (Lyric poetry: sed to be read ταρφύς τις μ.); μ. πόλεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 913 (Lyric poetry).}}

3462:
** μῠρίζω**,
**rub with ointment** or **unguent, anoint**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 938, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Comedy texts 23; μύροισιν μ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 529}}:—_middle_, **anoint oneself**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 148.5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 518.15; ἐξ ἀλαβάστου 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 62.1}}:—_passive_, μεμυρις μένοι τὸ σῶμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.195, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 190.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 832a4; μυρίζεσθαι τὴν κεφαλήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}} 2.142a.
**II** in _passive_ also, μ. τινί **to be fragrant with**.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.26. (Cf. σμυρίζω.)}}

3463:
Related to: ** μῡρῐ-άς**, δος, ἡ,
_Attic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ μυριαδῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 1.428}}:—**number of** {{Refs|10, 000}}**myriad**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 91, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 3.2, etc.; μ. πρώτη 10, 000 and μ. δευτέρα 10, 000}} **times** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|10, 000, 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 5.8}}: frequently of **countless numbers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 830 (Lyric poetry), etc.; μυριάδες ἐτῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 93}}; of money (i.e. δραχμῶν), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 829 (_anapaest meter_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 44}}; of corn (i.e. μεδίμνων), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.91, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.32}}; also (i.e. ἀρταβῶν), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1259.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** as _adjective_, φύστις μ. ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 927 (Lyric poetry: sed to be read ταρφύς τις μ.); μ. πόλεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 913 (Lyric poetry).}}

3464:
** μύρον** [ῠ], τό,
**sweet oil, unguent, perfume**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 31 (_plural_), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 36, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.18, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 9, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 14 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22; μύρον ἑψῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 946; ὄζω μύρου [prev. author] “Ec.” 524; μ. κατὰ τῆς κεφαλῆς καταχέαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a}}; mixed with wine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.31}}; various kinds in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.42ff, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.688e ff.}}; μ. Μενδήσιον, ἠθητόν, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 89.3, 436.1 (3rd c.BC): proverbial, τὸ ἐπὶ τῇ φακῇ μ.}} **sweet oil** on lentils, i.e. 'a jewel of gold in a swine's snout', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 1.19.2, compare 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 45, 4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 14, title of Menippean Satire by Varro.}}
**2** place where unguents were sold, **perfume-market**, τὰ μειράκια.. τἀν τῷ μ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1375, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Polyzelus Comicus 11; οἱ δ᾽ ἐν τῷ μ. λαλεῖτε 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 2; ἵσταται πρὸς τῷ μ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 209.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **anything graceful** or **charming,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 5.89.}}

3466:
** μυστήρι-ον**, τό,
(μύστης, μυέω) **mystery** or **secret rite**: mostly in _plural_, τὰ μ. **the mysteries**, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 14, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51}} (of **the mysteries** of the Cabiri in Samothrace), etc.; especially those of Demeter at Eleusis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 479, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 804, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 887, etc.}}; μυστηρίοις τοῖς μείζοσιν, τοῖς ὀλείζοσιν μ., {{Refs|“IG” 12.6.93,96}}; but usually, τὰ μεγάλα, τὰ μικρὰ μ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 846, compare “IG” 12.313.144, 22.1672.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497c, etc.; πρὸ τῶν μεγάλων μ. τὰ μικρὰ παραδοτέον 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 2}}; ἀπιέναι πρὸ τῶν μ., i.e. before you have reached the heart of the matter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e}}; τὰ τῆς θεοῦ (i.e. Μεγάλης Μητρὸς) μ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 540.21 (Pessinus, 1st c.AD)}}; οἱ θεοὶ οἷς τὰ μ. ἐπιτελεῖται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.2 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; τὰ μ. ποιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.11, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.42, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28 (_passive_)}}; μ. ἐρεῖν And.[same place]: later in _singular_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PMag.Leid.W.” 3.42 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **mystic implements** and **ornaments**, σεμνὰ στεμμάτων μυστήρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 470}}; especially **properties**, such as were carried to Eleusis at the celebration of the mysteries, ὄνος ἄγω μυστήρια, proverbial of an over-loaded beast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 159.}}
**2.b** later, **object used in magical rites, talisman**, δότε πνεῦμα τῷ ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ κατεσκευασμένῳ μ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PMag.Leid.V.” 10.19 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ὕπνος, τὰ μικρὰ τοῦ θανάτου μ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 11}}; τῆς Ῥωμαίων ἀρχῆς σεμνὸν μ., of the military **sacramentum**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.7.4}}: generally, **mystery, secret**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 156a; μυστήριόν σου μὴ κατείπῃς τῷ φίλῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 695, cf. LXX.Sir.27.16, LXX.Tob.12.7}}; προσήγγειλε τὰ μ. τοῖς πολεμίοις{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.2Mac.13.21; μυστήρια βίου 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.3}}; τοὐμὸν τὸ μ. [the remedy] is my **secret**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.7}}: hence, of a medicine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.96, 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 5.4.}}
**4** **secret revealed by God**, i.e. **religious** or **mystical truth, Corp. Herm.**{{Refs|1.16}}, etc.; τὰ μ. τῆς βασιλείας τῶν οὐρανῶν {{NT|NT.Matt.13.11; πνεύματι λαλεῖν μυστήρια NT.1Cor.14.2}}; τὸ μ. τῆς ἀνομίας the **mystery** of iniquity, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.2Thes.2.7; τὸν Ἀντιπάτρου βίον οὐκ ἂν ἁμάρτοιτις εἰπὼν κακίας μ. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.24.1}}; especially of the Gospel or parts of it, τὸ μ. τοῦ εὐαγγελίου {{NT|NT.Eph.6.19, compare 3.9, NT.Col.1.26, +others}}; **symbol**, τὸ μ. τῶν ἑπτὰ ἀστέρων {{NT|NT.Rev.1.20, compare 17.7.}}
**5** σύνηθές τι μ. some vulgar **superstition**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}
**II** Dionysius the tyrant called **mouse-holes** μυστήρια (μῦς, τηρεῖν), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.98d.}}

3470:
** μῶμος**, ὁ,
**blame, reproach, disgrace**, μῶμον ἀνάψαι to set a **brand** upon one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.86, compare 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.84, 105, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.74, “P.” 1.82; βροτῶν μ. πάντεσσι.. ἐστὶν ἐπ᾽ ἔργοις 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.202, compare “APl.” 4.84; οὐ γὰρ ἔσθ᾽ ὅπως σπουδῆς δικαίας μ. ἅψεταί ποτε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 257}}: also in later Prose, as {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Sir.11.31, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 5.20.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.820a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 20.}}
**2** **blemish**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.24.19, +others, NT.2Pet.2.13.}}
**II** personified, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Momus”, first in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 214, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487a, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 59.6.}}

3471:
** μωρ-αίνω**,
_future_ - ᾰνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 614}}: _aorist_ ἐμώρᾱνα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 719 (troch.):}} —_passive_, see below {{Refs|11}}: (μῶρος):—**to be silly, foolish, drivel**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.11, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.103K., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 45, etc.}}; **play the fool**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1148b2: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πεῖραν μ.}} **make a mad** attempt, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place]}}; οὐδεὶς.. ταῦτα μωραίνει **indulges in** these **follies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 282.22}}: euphemistic of illicit love, γυναῖκα μωραίνουσαν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 674.}}
**II** causal, **make foolish, convict of folly**, ἡ βουλὴ αὐτῶν μωρανθήσεται {{LXX|LXX.Isa.19.11}}; ἐμωράνθην σφόδρα{{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.24.10, cf. NT.1Cor.1.20}}:—_passive_, **to become foolish, be stupefied**, [αἶγες] ἑστᾶσιν ὥσπερ μεμωραμμέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610b30 (sed compare μωρόομαι)}}; **to become insipid**, ἐὰν τὸ ἅλας μωρανθῇ {{NT|NT.Matt.5.13.}}

3474:
** μωρός**, ά, όν,
_Attic dialect_ **μῶρος** Hdn.Gr. 1.192: μῶρος as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 61}}, but _accusative_ μῶραν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.17 (assuming variant)}}:—**dull, sluggish**, of the nerves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 2}}; χειμῶνος ἀρχομένου μ. γίνονται οἱ ἐργάται [τῶν σφηκῶν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 628a6.}}
**2** of persons, **dull, stupid**, μωροῦ φωτὸς ἅδε βουλά {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 57.6, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 220, 470, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.94, LXX.Sir.18.18, +others, NT.Matt.7.26, etc.}}: _comparative_ in proverbial, μωρότερος Μορύχου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 74}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.22.}}
**2.b** in a religious sense, λαὸς μ. καὶ ἀκάρδιος {{LXX|LXX.Jer.5.21, +others}}
**3** of things, δέδοικα μῶρον πυραύστου μόρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 288; μῶρόν ἐστι τοὐγχείρημά σου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 540}}; τὸ μ. **folly**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 966; τὸ μ. τοῦ θεοῦ σοφώτερον τῶν ἀνθρώπων ἐστί NT.1Cor.1.25}}; μῶρα φρονεῖν, φωνεῖν, δρᾶν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 594, “OT” 433, “Ant.” 469; μῶρα.. μῶρος λέγει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 369, cf. LXX.Isa.32.6; μ. βουλεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 474.}}
**3.b** μ. ἀνάγκη **blind** necessity, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 101 G.}}
**4** of taste, **insipid, flat, Comedy texts Adesp.**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|596, Diocl.fragment 138, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.19.}}
**II** _adverb_ -ρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.21}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _mūrás_ 'idiot'.)

3483:
** ναί**,
_adverb_, used to express strong affirmation, **yea, verily**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly followed by δή, ν. δὴ ταῦτά γε πάντα.. κατὰ μοῖραν ἔειπες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.286, +others; ν. μήν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 76.2; ν. μάν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.27; ν. μέν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.151; ν. μέντοι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Astr.” 14: used alone, σὲ κρίνω, ν. σέ}} **yea** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1445; ἀποκρίνασθαι ν. ἢ οὔ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 158a16, etc.; τὸ ε ¯ οὐκ ἔστι χαρακτηριστικὸν.., τὸ δὲ ᾱ ναί 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.85.}}
**2** frequently in oaths, ν. μά **yea by**.., see at {μ; ν}. alone, ν. τὰν Κόραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1438, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 534 (Lyric poetry); ν. τὸν Ποτιδᾶν 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 81; ν. τὼ σιώ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.10}}; ν. πρὸς θεῶν ἀρήξατ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1277.}}
**II** in answers, **yes**, τοῦτ᾽ ἐτήτυμο; Answ. ν. {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 738 (troch.), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 845 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 193a, “Grg.” 448b, etc.; doubled, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1468, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 1.56 P., NT.Matt.5.37.}}
**II.2** ν. followed by ἀλλά, etc., to mark a qualified assent, **yes**, but.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.84}}; ν., κατὰ σχολήν γε ἴσως· οὐ μήν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 226e, compare “R.” 415e.}}

3485:
** νᾱός**, ὁ,
_Doric dialect_, Thess., etc. form, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 1.42, “IG” 9(2).517.45 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC), etc., used also in Trag. (even dialogue) to the exclusion of νεώς, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 8, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 31, +others, except 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 810}}, rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose and Comedy texts, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 394a, “Lg.” 738c, 814b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1174a24, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 29.1, more frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, “HG” 2.3.20, “An.” 5.3.9, +others, found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr.from 3rd c.BC, “IG” 22.1314.18, 1315.28, etc., and in Hellenistic and later Gr. (along with νεώς), “SIG” 277 (Priene, 4th c.BC), 214 (Phanagoria, 4th c.BC), 494.3 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), LXX.1Ki.1.9, +others (νεώς}} only in **2 Ma.)**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 6.22 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.30.2 (νεώς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.4.4), etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_**νηός**, always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), but _genitive_ νε[ώ] “IG” 12(7).1.4 (Amorgos, 5th c.BC)}}; _dative_ νειῴ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Michel” 832.38 (Samos, 4th c.BC)}}; _Attic dialect_ **νεώς** (Attic Inscrr. of see-3rd c.BC (see. below), once in Trag. (see. above), frequently in Prose authors and found in Comedy texts (see. below)); declension, _nominative_ νεώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.1; _genitive_ νεώ “IG” 12.4.9,80.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 733, “IG” 22.1524.45, “SIG” 1219.32 (Gambreum, 3rd c.BC)}}; _dative_ νεῴ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.6.122, 256.4, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.39, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 40.3, “IG” 22.1504.7}}; _accusative_ νεών [{{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. work]12.24.13, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 401, “Pl.” 741, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 139, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 340, later νεώ “IG” 22.212.35 (4th c.BC), +others, LXX.2Mac.6.2, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.58 (variant{νεών}), “SIG” 877 A 10 (2nd-3rd c.AD), variant in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.26, but νεών 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 27(16).19 (variant{νεώ}), 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 3.6 (variant{νεώ} Bast “Epist.Crit.” p.176), etc.: _plural_ _nominative_ νεῴ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.7; _accusative_ νεώς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 810, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.117, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.4.4; _dative_ νεῴς “IG” 12.384}}]; on the accent see Hdn.Gr.1.8: _Aeolic dialect_ **ναῦος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 9, “IG” 12 (2).60.27 (Mytil.)}}; Spartan **ναϝός** [prev. work]5(1).1564 (_plural_, found at Delos, 5th-4th c.BC):—**temple**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.39, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.21 (_plural_), etc.}}
**II** **inmost part of a temple, shrine** containing the image of the god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.183, 6.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 15, “UPZ” [same place]; ἐν παντὶ ἱερῷ ὅπου ναός ἐστι “PGnom.” 79 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** **portable shrine** carried in processions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.63, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.15, etc.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, of Christians, ν. θεοῦ ἐστε {{NT|NT.1Cor.3.16}}; of the body of Christ, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.John.2.19, 21. [νᾰόν and νᾰῶ 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32b iv (Phaestus, 2nd c.BC)}}; elsewhere ᾱ.] (Perh. from νᾰσ-ϝός, compare ναίω.)

3487:
** νάρδος**, ἡ,
**spikenard, Nardostachys Jatamansi**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.7.2, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 604, LXX.Cant.1.12, NT.Mark.14.3; ν. Ἰνδική 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.7, etc.; νάρδου στάχυς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.84, +others}}; compare {ναρδόσταχυς}
**2** ν. Κελτική **Celtic nard, Valeriana celtica**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.8, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 14.107.}}
**3** ν. ὀρεινή or ὀρεία **mountain nard, Valeriana Dioscoridis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.9 (compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.7.4).}}
**4** ν. Συριακή **Syrian nard, Cymbopogon Iwarancusa**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.7, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 12.45.}}
**5** νάρδου ῥίζα **ginger grass, Cymbopogon Schoenanthus**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.22, compare 7.20.}}
**6** ν. ἀγρία, ={ἄσαρον}, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.10; ={φοῦ}, [prev. work]11.}}]
**II** **oil of spikenard**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 6.628.7 (3rd c.BC), “Anthologia Graeca” 6.250 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “χρονίων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.2, etc.; ν. Βαβυλωνιακή 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 308}}. (Semitic word, cf. Bab. **lardu.**)

3490:
** ναύκληρ-ος**, ὁ,
**shipowner and merchant** (opposed to ἔμπορος, which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.127.34, 128.4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 4.152, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 128, 547, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 417, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 595, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.11.}}
**2** **skipper, sailing-master**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 344.4 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), NT.Act.27.11, “POxy.” 63.4 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**3** generally, **captain, commander**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 177}}: as _adjective_, ν. πλάτη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 430}}; ν. χείρ **the master's** hand, of a charioteer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1224; ν. πόλις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.26.2.}}
**II** at Athens, **one who rented and sub-let tenement-houses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sannyrio Comicus 6, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 37, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 37, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.75.}}
**II.2** ={ναύκραρος}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.108 (assuming variant)}}; compare ναύκλαρος.

3492:
** ναύτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(ναῦς) **seaman, sailor,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.375, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 9.8, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.188, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302a, etc.}}: as _adjective_, ν. ὅμιλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 921 (Lyric poetry)}}; **by sea,** opposed to πεζός (by land), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 719 (troch.).}}
**II** **passenger by sea,** ναύτην ἄγειν τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 901: _metaphorically_, συμποσίου ναῦται}} **mates** in the drinking bout, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Dionysius Chalcus Elegiacus 5.}}

3494:
** νεᾱν-ίας**, ου,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **νεηνίης**, εω, ὁ, _dative_ _plural_ νεανίοις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).205.25 (Phthiotis, 3rd c.BC)}}: (νεάν):—**young man,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”) always with ἀνήρ, νεηνίῃ ἀνδρὶ ἐοικώς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.278; ἄνδρες κοιμήσαντο νεηνίαι 14.524; παῖδες νεηνίαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, compare 7.99; ν. γαμβρός 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.4; τέκτονες κώμων ν. [prev. author] “N.” 3.5}}: without a _substantive_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37, 43, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 335, “El.” 750, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 698, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.1.2, etc.}}
**2** frequently with the sense of **a youth** in character, i.e. either in good sense, **impetuous, active,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1041, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1333, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.313}}; or in bad sense, **hot-headed, wilful, headstrong,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 580; ἓν μὲν τοίνυν τοῦτο.. πολίτευμα τοῦ νεανίου τούτου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.136, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239d.}}
**II** as _masculine_ _adjective_, **youthful,** νεανίαι τὰς ὄψεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.29.}}
**II.2** of things, etc., **new, young, fresh,** νεανίαις ὤμοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1562; ν. θώρακα καὶ βραχίονα [prev. author] “HF” 1095; ἄρτος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1207}}; ν. λόγοι **rash, wilful** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 679. [νεανιῶν}} is _trisyllable_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1069}}; compare νεανικός.]

3495:
** νεᾱνισκ-ος**,
_Ionic dialect_ **νεην-**, ὁ, (νεάν) **youth, young man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.374.162, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72, 112, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b21, Anon.Hist.(“FGrH” 160) p.887 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.}}; ν. τὸ εἶδος (nisi to be read νεανικός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.5; in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53 the same youth is called both νεηνίης and νεηνίσκος}}; but in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.6 and 8 the same person is called ν. and μειράκιον}} and the eldest son of Socrates is called νεανίσκος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.1, but μειράκιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 34d; ἔν τε παισὶ καὶ ν. καὶ ἐν ἀνδράσι [prev. author] “R.” 413e; παῖς εἴκοσι ἔτεα, νεηνίσκος εἴκοσι, νεηνίης εἴκοσι, γέρων εἴκοσι 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.10, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑβδομάδων - Die Hippokratische Schrift von der Siebenzahl” 5: more frequently than νεανίας}} in later Gr., Wilcken {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 30i4 (3rd-2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.36.12, NT.Matt.19.20, etc.}}
**2** ὁ ἐμὸς ν. my **young man,** i.e. **servant,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 53; perhaps also, “PCair.Zen.” 18.6 (3rd c.BC), NT.Mark.14.51: _plural_, uncertain meaning, “PCair.Zen.” 153 (3rd c.BC, written νενίσκοις).}}

3498:
** νεκρός**, ὁ
(of a woman, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 129)}}, **corpse,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: as _substantive_, in early writers always of mankind, νεκροὺς συλήσετε τεθνηῶτας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.71; ν. ἔρυον κατατεθνηῶτας 18.540}}: frequently of those killed in battle, τοὺς ν. ὑποσπόνδους ἀνείλοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44, etc.: in _sing._, νεκρῷ ἐούσῃ Μελίσσῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. η; κεῖται ν. περὶ νεκρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1240, etc.; Πατρόκλῳ ν. ὄντι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391b: the _Article_ is frequently omitted even of a particular corpse, especially when a _genitive_ is added, ν. γυναικός, ἀνθρώπου, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 90, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 659, “Th.” 1018}}; later, of a fish, ν. ἰχθύος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 6.13: _neuter_ _plural_ νεκρά, τά, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.773d: _metaphorically_, νεκρὰ καὶ καπνός 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 12.33.}}
**2** **dying person,** μυχθισμὸς νεκρῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 789; ν. ἀσπαίροντες 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.5; ν. ἀποθνῄσκοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ὁ υἱός μου ν. ἦν καὶ ἀνέζησε {{NT|NT.Luke.15.24; ὄνομα ἔχεις ὅτι ζῇς καὶ ν. εἶ NT.Rev.3.1; ν. τῇ ἁμαρτίᾳ NT.Rom.6.11.}}
**4** in _plural_, **the dead,** as dwellers in the nether world, κλυτὰ ἔθνεα νεκρῶν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.526, compare 11.34, etc.; ἐν νεκροῖς LXX.Psa.87(88).5; ἐκ νεκρῶν ἐγεῖραι NT.John.12.1; ἡ ἀνάστασις ἡ ἐκ ν. NT.Luke.20.35: _metaphorically_, ζωὴ ἐκ ν. NT.Rom.11.15.}}
**II** as _adjective_ νεκρός, ά, όν, **dead,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, ν. ἵππος “fragment” 203}}; ν. σώματα Mitteis {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 ii 22 (2nd c.BC), 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 3.5, compare 1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 58 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.685b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus Eph.5.1, “POxy.” 51.8 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 1024vii26 (4th-5th c.AD)}} (but also τὰ τῶν ν. σώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959b; σῶμα.. νεκροῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 679); ν. χελώνη 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 7.4}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.135 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** **inanimate, inorganic,** opposed to ἔμψυχος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.6}}; οὐχὶ ν., ὥσπερ λίθον ἢ ξύλον, ἀλλὰ ζῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.7.9; οὐρανὸς.. ὢν πρὸ ψυχῆς σῶμα ν., γῆ καὶ ὕδωρ [prev. author] 5.1.2}}; ἡ ν. θάλασσα the {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Dead” Sea, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.7.4, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.690, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1082.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, ν. πλοῦτος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.1.}}

3499:
** νεκρ-όω**,
**make dead, mortify,** μόριόν τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.265}}:—_passive_, νενεκρῶσθαι τὸ μόριον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 18(1).156: _metaphorically_, τὰ δόγματα.. δύναται νεκρωθῆναι 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.2; οὐ ψυχὴ κυρίως, ἀλλὰ νενεκρωμένη τις 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 1066.27}}; **to be dead,** νεκρωθείς {{Refs|“IG” 14.1976}}; νενεκρωμένος, of the body of Abraham, {{NT|NT.Rom.4.19.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **mortify,** νεκρώσατε τὰ μέλη {{NT|NT.Col.3.5.}}
